/// \defgroup grp_w1019_w3d_render_stats_device W3D: frame statistics, terrain-track buffers, motion-blur setup and screen coordinates
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief The Debug_Statistics frame counters and texture report, the
///        terrain-track system's buffer rebuild, the motion-blur screen
///        filter's per-mode setup, and the pixel-to-W3D screen conversion.
///
/// Four small, settled pieces of the W3D engine (\ref sub_w3d_rendering),
/// each at its own seam:
///
/// - Frame statistics. Debug_Statistics is the WW3D2 module that counts
///   what each rendered frame cost: polygons, vertices and draw calls, plus
///   texture tallies that nothing feeds in BFME 2 retail.
///   Debug_Statistics::Begin_Statistics opens a frame,
///   Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics freezes the counts, and the getters
///   return the frozen values of the last finished frame.
/// - Terrain tracks. TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem draws the track
///   marks on the terrain from one shared vertex buffer and one shared
///   index buffer. TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::ReAcquireResources
///   rebuilds those two buffers, after a Direct3D device reset that reloads
///   assets among other times.
/// - Motion blur. ScreenMotionBlurFilter is the screen filter behind the
///   camera motion-blur effects. ScreenMotionBlurFilter::setup turns a
///   filter mode into the filter's working settings when the effect starts.
/// - Screen coordinates. PixelScreenToW3DLogicalScreen converts a pixel
///   position into the -1 to 1 coordinates that W3D cameras unproject.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The size of the terrain-track vertex buffer comes from game data. It
///   holds two vertices per track edge for as many tracks as the GlobalData
///   track count allows, and BFME 2's retail GlobalData field table fills
///   that count from the MaxTerrainTracks field of GameData.ini. The edge
///   count per track is a separate limit; Zero Hour takes it from the
///   MaxTankTrackEdges field of the static LOD definitions (GameLOD.ini),
///   and BFME 2's static LOD field table has the same field name. See
///   TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::ReAcquireResources for the
///   arithmetic.
/// - The camera motion-blur effects are driven by map scripts. BFME 2's
///   action template table defines CAMERA_MOTION_BLUR,
///   CAMERA_MOTION_BLUR_JUMP, CAMERA_MOTION_BLUR_FOLLOW and
///   CAMERA_MOTION_BLUR_END_FOLLOW, and the editor hint for the follow
///   action's amount reads "start with 30 and adjust up or down". In Zero
///   Hour the follow action's amount becomes the filter's pan factor, so 30
///   matches the default that ScreenMotionBlurFilter::setup falls back to;
///   that BFME 2 maps the amount the same way is not established.
/// - Nothing in Debug_Statistics is data-driven.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - PixelScreenToW3DLogicalScreen is where pixel space meets the camera's
///   normalised space, and its callers decide which pixel space that is.
///   Its callers include the view's pick ray, which passes a position
///   relative to the view's origin with the view's own size, and the 3D
///   mouse cursor, which passes the raw cursor position with the whole
///   display's size. A port that adds resolution scaling, letterboxing or
///   high-DPI input must keep the pixel space and the width and height
///   consistent at every call site.
/// - The terrain-track buffers are rebuilt from the device-reset path:
///   after a successful reset that is asked to reload assets,
///   DX8Wrapper::Reset_Device calls its cleanup hook,
///   BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::ReAcquireResources, which calls
///   TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::ReAcquireResources. The hook's
///   release half runs on every reset. A reset without asset reload relies
///   on BFME 2's TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::flush, which re-creates
///   the buffers when either is missing the next time flush runs with a
///   track in use (outside the reflection pass).
/// - The motion-blur filter needs render-to-texture. Its init function
///   enters it in W3DShaderManager's filter table only when
///   W3DShaderManager::canRenderToTexture reports support, so on hardware
///   without it the effect is unavailable. Its zoom effects advance once
///   per logic frame, so their speed follows the logic rate, not the
///   render rate.
/// - Debug_Statistics keeps its counters in plain global integers with no
///   locking. The recorders add to them during drawing and
///   Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics copies them into the last-frame
///   values, which assumes all rendering runs on one thread.


/// \file Common/W3DConvert.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_w3d_render_stats_device
/// \brief PixelScreenToW3DLogicalScreen, the pixel to W3D logical screen
///        conversion.
///
/// Ported from the Open-BFME-1 unit of the same name, which is Zero Hour's
/// W3DConvert.cpp. Zero Hour's file also holds the inverse,
/// W3DLogicalScreenToPixelScreen.


/// \namespace Debug_Statistics
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_w3d_render_stats_device
/// \brief Per-frame render statistics: polygon, vertex and draw-call
///        counters, texture usage tallies and a text report of the
///        textures a frame used.
///
/// Debug_Statistics is Westwood's WW3D2 statistics module, the same in
/// outline as Zero Hour's statistics.cpp and statistics.h. Zero Hour
/// declares it as a namespace of free functions over file-level counters.
/// Retail code cannot show whether BFME 2 kept the namespace or used a
/// class with static member functions.
///
/// \par How a frame is measured
/// - Debug_Statistics::Begin_Statistics clears the per-frame counters and
///   starts texture recording. In BFME 2 retail one caller is a WW3D
///   function this guide does not name and the other is
///   DX8Wrapper::Reset_Device, which calls it during a device reset, after
///   the device resources are released. Zero Hour calls it from WW3D's
///   Begin_Render and also from W3DDisplay::draw, before the render-target
///   pass (see below).
/// - While the frame draws, the recorders add to the counters:
///   Debug_Statistics::Record_DX8_Polys_And_Vertices (polygons, vertices
///   and a draw call), Debug_Statistics::Record_DX8_Skin_Polys_And_Vertices
///   (skinned polygons and vertices and a skinned render) and
///   Debug_Statistics::Record_Sorting_Polys_And_Vertices (sorted polygons
///   and vertices and a draw call). Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture is the
///   module's texture recorder, but in BFME 2 retail nothing calls it (see
///   below).
/// - Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics copies the counters into last-frame
///   values. In BFME 2 retail its one direct caller is a WW3D function this
///   guide does not name. Zero Hour calls it from WW3D's End_Render and
///   also from W3DDisplay::draw, after the render-target pass.
/// - The getters (Get_DX8_Polygons, Get_Draw_Calls, Get_Record_Texture_Size
///   and the others) return those last-frame values, so a reader sees the
///   counters as of the last End_Statistics, never a count in progress.
///
/// Zero Hour: W3DDisplay::draw calls Begin_Statistics before updating the
/// views and the water and projected-shadow render-target textures, and
/// End_Statistics after them. It then reads the polygon and vertex counts
/// and, once WW3D's Begin_Render has reset the counters again, adds them
/// back with Record_DX8_Polys_And_Vertices, so the frame's totals include
/// the render-target pass. Zero Hour therefore freezes the counters twice
/// per frame, and between the two freezes its getters return the
/// render-target pass alone. In BFME 2 retail Begin_Statistics and
/// End_Statistics have no direct callers besides the ones described above, so
/// this second pair of calls has no direct BFME 2 counterpart.
///
/// DX8Wrapper keeps its own set of state-change counters (matrix, material,
/// vertex and index buffer, light, texture, render-state and texture-stage
/// changes, Direct3D calls and draw calls). Begin_Statistics and
/// End_Statistics end by calling DX8Wrapper::Begin_Statistics and
/// DX8Wrapper::End_Statistics, which reset and freeze that set the same
/// way.
///
/// \par Texture recording modes
/// The mode set by Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture_Mode decides how much
/// Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture records each time it is called. The
/// list describes BFME 2's Record_Texture body, but in BFME 2 retail no code
/// calls it, so nothing is recorded in any mode and the texture tallies stay
/// 0 (see "Who feeds and reads the statistics" below). The mode then only
/// decides whether Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics builds the texture
/// report. Zero Hour: TextureClass::Apply calls Record_Texture each time it
/// applies a texture, so there the modes below describe live tallies.
/// - RECORD_TEXTURE_NONE: count texture binds and texture changes only.
/// - RECORD_TEXTURE_SIMPLE: also record each distinct texture once, with
///   its memory use, and count lightmap and procedural textures separately.
///   In BFME 2 both the lightmap test and the procedural test call a
///   shared function that always returns false, so the lightmap and
///   procedural counts and memory are always 0. Zero Hour asks the texture.
/// - RECORD_TEXTURE_DETAILS: also count each texture's uses and changes,
///   and at the end of the frame build the text report that
///   Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_String returns.
///
/// \par BFME 2 compared with Zero Hour
/// Established from the BFME 2 bodies and the retail call graph:
/// - BFME 2 resets eleven per-frame counters where Zero Hour resets eight.
///   Eight are identified by the recorders that add to them; the roles of
///   the other three are not established.
/// - The skinned-render recorder does not count a draw call in BFME 2;
///   Zero Hour's does.
/// - Recorded textures are held as reference-counted texture pointers
///   (RefCountPtr of TextureClass) rather than raw pointers.
/// - With recording set to RECORD_TEXTURE_NONE, BFME 2's Record_Texture
///   forgets the previous texture after each call, so each later bind of a
///   texture counts as a change; Zero Hour's remembers it and counts only
///   real changes.
/// - In BFME 2 both the lightmap test and the procedural test call a
///   shared function that always returns false, so the lightmap and
///   procedural counts and memory are always 0. Zero Hour asks the texture.
/// - In BFME 2 retail no code calls Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture, so
///   every texture tally stays 0. Zero Hour calls it from
///   TextureClass::Apply.
///
/// \par Who feeds and reads the statistics
/// In BFME 2 retail no code calls Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture. A scan
/// of the retail image finds no direct call to it and no stored address of
/// it, and its two helpers, which find and add a recorded texture, are
/// called only from it. The texture tallies (memory, count, binds and
/// changes) are touched only by the statistics module's
/// Record_Texture_Begin, which zeroes them, its Record_Texture_End, which
/// copies them into the last-frame values, and Record_Texture together with
/// its two helpers (which only Record_Texture calls). They therefore stay 0
/// every frame:
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Size,
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Count and
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Change_Count always return 0, as do
/// the lightmap and procedural getters, and a detail-mode report holds only
/// its header, with zero counts, and its legend. Zero Hour:
/// TextureClass::Apply calls Record_Texture each time it applies a texture,
/// so there the tallies are live.
///
/// The same scan finds direct calls to
/// Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture_Mode and
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Size from code this guide does not
/// identify, and no direct call or stored address for the other texture
/// getters. The code that calls Get_Record_Texture_Size formats the result
/// into a "Video RAM: %d" line, so in BFME 2 retail that readout shows 0.
/// Zero Hour calls Record_Texture_Mode and Get_Record_Texture_Size from two
/// functions in W3DDisplay.cpp: W3DDisplay's gatherDebugStats, which builds
/// the on-screen debug statistics, and StatDumpClass's dumpStats, which writes
/// a statistics dump file and is compiled only when DUMP_PERF_STATS is
/// defined. No Zero Hour code reads the other texture getters.


/// \fn void Debug_Statistics::Begin_Statistics()
/// \brief Starts a statistics frame: clears the per-frame render counters,
///        restarts texture recording and resets DX8Wrapper's counters.
///
/// The counters cleared are the ones the recorders add to while the frame
/// draws: DX8 polygons, vertices and draw calls, skinned polygons, vertices
/// and renders, sorted polygons and vertices, and three counters whose role
/// is not established. Texture recording restarts through the
/// statistics module's Record_Texture_Begin, which clears the texture
/// tallies, forgets the previous texture and empties the list of recorded
/// textures. Last comes DX8Wrapper::Begin_Statistics. The last-frame values
/// the getters return are not touched, so they keep describing the previous
/// frame until Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics runs.
///
/// In BFME 2 retail one caller is a WW3D function this guide does not name;
/// the other is DX8Wrapper::Reset_Device, which calls it while the device
/// is being reset, after releasing the device resources. A reset therefore
/// also discards whatever the current frame had counted so far. Zero Hour
/// calls it from WW3D's Begin_Render and also from W3DDisplay::draw, before
/// the render-target pass; the Debug_Statistics page describes that second
/// call.
///
/// \note Zero Hour clears eight counters here; BFME 2 clears eleven.
/// \see Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics, DX8Wrapper::Begin_Statistics


/// \fn void Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics()
/// \brief Ends a statistics frame: freezes the frame's counters into the
///        last-frame values the getters return.
///
/// The texture tallies are frozen first, by the statistics module's
/// Record_Texture_End: texture memory and count, change count, and the
/// lightmap and procedural memory and counts become the values that
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Size and its siblings return (all
/// 0 in BFME 2 retail; see the Debug_Statistics page). In
/// RECORD_TEXTURE_DETAILS mode Record_Texture_End also rebuilds the
/// texture report (see Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_String);
/// otherwise it leaves the report empty. Then the polygon, vertex,
/// skinned-render, sorting and draw-call counters are copied for
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_DX8_Polygons, Debug_Statistics::Get_DX8_Vertices,
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_DX8_Skin_Renders,
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_DX8_Skin_Polygons,
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_DX8_Skin_Vertices,
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_Sorting_Polygons,
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_Sorting_Vertices and
/// Debug_Statistics::Get_Draw_Calls, and finally DX8Wrapper::End_Statistics
/// freezes DX8Wrapper's own counters.
///
/// It has one direct caller in BFME 2 retail, a WW3D function this guide
/// does not name. Zero Hour calls it from WW3D's End_Render and also from
/// W3DDisplay::draw, after the render-target pass, so Zero Hour freezes the
/// counters twice per frame; the Debug_Statistics page describes that
/// second call.
///
/// \note BFME 2 copies ten counters here: the eight above and two of the
///       three whose role is not established. The third is cleared by
///       Debug_Statistics::Begin_Statistics but never copied.
/// \see Debug_Statistics::Begin_Statistics, DX8Wrapper::End_Statistics


/// \fn void Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture_Mode(RecordTextureMode mode)
/// \brief Sets how much detail Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture records
///        about the textures a frame uses.
///
/// The modes are described on the Debug_Statistics page. The mode takes
/// effect for the next texture recorded; a report is built only by a
/// Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics that runs in RECORD_TEXTURE_DETAILS
/// mode.
///
/// Retail calls it with RECORD_TEXTURE_SIMPLE from one site in code this
/// guide does not identify. Zero Hour makes the same call, before reading
/// the texture memory, in both of its texture-statistics readers:
/// W3DDisplay's gatherDebugStats and StatDumpClass's dumpStats.
///
/// \param mode RECORD_TEXTURE_NONE, RECORD_TEXTURE_SIMPLE or
///             RECORD_TEXTURE_DETAILS.
/// \see Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Mode


/// \fn Debug_Statistics::RecordTextureMode Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Mode()
/// \brief Returns the texture recording mode set by
///        Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture_Mode.
/// \return The current mode.


/// \fn int Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Size()
/// \brief Returns the texture memory recorded in the last finished frame,
///        in bytes. Always 0 in BFME 2 retail.
///
/// The value is the sum of the memory use of each distinct texture that
/// Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture recorded during that frame, as frozen by
/// Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics. Nothing is summed in
/// RECORD_TEXTURE_NONE mode. In BFME 2 retail no code calls Record_Texture,
/// so nothing is recorded and the value is always 0 (see the
/// Debug_Statistics page).
///
/// Retail formats this value into a "Video RAM: %d" line in code this
/// guide does not identify, and prints it as returned, so that line shows
/// 0. Zero Hour prints the same line from W3DDisplay's gatherDebugStats (the
/// on-screen debug statistics) and from StatDumpClass's dumpStats (a
/// statistics dump file); both first subtract a fixed allowance for the
/// terrain alpha texture, and the value is live there because
/// TextureClass::Apply records each texture it applies.
///
/// \return Bytes of texture memory; 0 in BFME 2 retail.


/// \fn int Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Count()
/// \brief Returns the number of distinct textures recorded in the last
///        finished frame.
/// \return Texture count; 0 in RECORD_TEXTURE_NONE mode.
/// \note Always 0 in BFME 2 retail, because no code calls
///       Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture; see the Debug_Statistics page.
///       Zero Hour: TextureClass::Apply records each texture it applies,
///       so the count is live there.


/// \fn int Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_Change_Count()
/// \brief Returns how many recorded texture binds in the last finished
///        frame changed the texture, counted in every recording mode.
/// \return Number of texture changes.
/// \note Always 0 in BFME 2 retail, because no code calls
///       Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture; see the Debug_Statistics page.
///       Zero Hour: TextureClass::Apply records each texture it applies,
///       so the count is live there.


/// \fn int Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Lightmap_Texture_Size()
/// \brief Returns the memory used by the lightmap textures among the
///        last finished frame's recorded textures, in bytes.
/// \return Bytes of lightmap texture memory.
/// \note Always 0 in BFME 2; see the texture recording modes on the
///       Debug_Statistics page.


/// \fn int Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Lightmap_Texture_Count()
/// \brief Returns how many of the last finished frame's recorded textures
///        are lightmaps.
/// \return Lightmap texture count.
/// \note Always 0 in BFME 2; see the texture recording modes on the
///       Debug_Statistics page.


/// \fn int Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Procedural_Texture_Size()
/// \brief Returns the memory used by the procedural textures among the
///        last finished frame's recorded textures, in bytes.
/// \return Bytes of procedural texture memory.
/// \note Always 0 in BFME 2; see the texture recording modes on the
///       Debug_Statistics page.


/// \fn int Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Procedural_Texture_Count()
/// \brief Returns how many of the last finished frame's recorded textures
///        are procedural.
/// \return Procedural texture count.
/// \note Always 0 in BFME 2; see the texture recording modes on the
///       Debug_Statistics page.


/// \fn const StringClass &Debug_Statistics::Get_Record_Texture_String()
/// \brief Returns the texture report built at the end of the last frame
///        recorded in RECORD_TEXTURE_DETAILS mode.
///
/// Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics empties the report every frame and
/// refills it only in detail mode. The report, built by the statistics
/// module's Record_Texture_End, starts with the frame's texture bind count
/// and change count, then has one row per recorded texture: an id column
/// that is always 0, the texture's use and change counts, its memory in
/// kilobytes (an asterisk marks a texture that is not yet initialised,
/// N/A one with no texture object) and its name. A legend follows. The
/// legend still describes columns the rows no longer print (reduction
/// factor, size without reduction, percent saved) and an id for a
/// flash_texture command; Zero Hour's copy has the same legend.
///
/// In BFME 2 retail no code calls Debug_Statistics::Record_Texture, so
/// nothing is recorded and a detail-mode report holds only the header, with
/// zero counts, and the legend.
///
/// \return A reference to the module's report string. It stays valid for
///         the life of the program and changes at the next
///         Debug_Statistics::End_Statistics.


/// \class TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_w3d_render_stats_device
/// \brief The manager that owns every terrain track-mark render object and
///        draws them from one shared vertex buffer and index buffer.
///
/// Track marks are the ribbons of tread or wheel marks left on the ground
/// behind moving units. Each ribbon is a TerrainTracksRenderObjClass, a
/// chain of edges (pairs of points across the track).
/// TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::flush draws every track in use that
/// has at least two edges, two vertices per edge, with the edges past the
/// opaque limit fading out.
/// Zero Hour: the system keeps a fixed pool of track objects, hands them
/// out to draw modules and takes them back, and ages their edges each frame
/// in its update.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// Zero Hour: there is one system, TheTerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem,
/// created and initialised with the 3D scene by W3DTerrainVisual's init,
/// emptied by its reset and deleted by its destructor. W3DModelDraw binds a
/// track to its render object when its module data's TrackMarks field names
/// a texture and GlobalData's track-marks switch is on, and unbinds it in its
/// destructor. In BFME 2 the terrain's device-reset hook tests a global
/// pointer to the system before calling into it; the rest of the BFME 2
/// lifecycle is not described here.
///
/// The system's state includes:
/// - the shared dynamic vertex buffer, sized for every track at its
///   maximum length, and the shared index buffer, which describes one track
///   of maximum length; both are created by
///   TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::ReAcquireResources;
/// - the vertex material and shader that flush draws the tracks with,
///   which the init-shaped body described under ReAcquireResources sets
///   from the prelit-diffuse material preset and the alpha shader preset;
/// - a list of tracks in use and a list of free ones;
/// - the track limits copied from GlobalData: the maximum edges per track,
///   the number of edges that stay fully opaque before the rest fade, and a
///   fade delay.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// - The index buffer depends only on the maximum edges per track, and the
///   vertex buffer on that limit and the track count, so both must be
///   rebuilt whenever either changes.
/// - BFME 2's flush re-creates both buffers when either is missing, on any
///   call that has at least one track in use and is not the reflection
///   pass, even if no track then has enough edges to draw. It returns
///   before that test when no track is in use, and when a
///   camera-projection override flag is set, which the source reads as the
///   reflection pass (Zero Hour also skips track marks in reflections).
///   Buffers that a release left missing are therefore restored by the
///   next such flush, not by the next frame as such.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The system is a Direct3D resource owner on the device-reset path; see
/// TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::ReAcquireResources.


/// \fn void TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::ReAcquireResources(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_w3d_render_stats_device
/// \brief Releases and re-creates the shared index and vertex buffers that
///        all terrain track marks are drawn from.
///
/// Both buffers are Direct3D resources, so they have to be rebuilt after the
/// device is reset and whenever the edge limits change. In BFME 2 retail the
/// direct calls to it come from four functions:
/// - BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::ReAcquireResources, the terrain's
///   device-reset hook, which DX8Wrapper::Reset_Device calls after a
///   successful reset when it is asked to reload assets (the hook's release
///   half runs on every reset);
/// - TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::flush, when either buffer is
///   missing; after a reset without asset reload, this is what re-creates
///   the buffers, the next time flush runs with a track in use (outside
///   the reflection pass);
/// - two functions of the track system whose bodies follow Zero Hour's init
///   and setDetail. The first stores the scene, rebuilds the buffers, sets
///   the material and shader, and fills the pool of free tracks when both
///   track lists are empty. The second, after two clean-up calls,
///   copies three track limits from GlobalData (the maximum edges per
///   track, the opaque-edge count and a fade delay) and then rebuilds.
///
/// Zero Hour has three callers: the system's init, at start-up; its
/// setDetail, after the detail settings change the edge limits; and
/// BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::ReAcquireResources, the same device-reset
/// hook. Zero Hour's flush never calls it, so the on-demand rebuild from
/// flush is BFME 2's alone.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Any existing buffers are released first and their pointers cleared,
///   so calling it again is safe.
/// - The index buffer holds six 16-bit indices for each pair of
///   neighbouring edges of one maximum-length track: edges i and i+1 make a
///   quad from vertices 2i, 2i+1, 2i+2 and 2i+3, drawn as two triangles.
///   Every track reuses this one index list.
/// - The vertex buffer is a dynamic buffer of position, diffuse colour and
///   one texture coordinate per vertex, with room for two vertices per edge
///   for the maximum number of tracks: track count times maximum edges times
///   two. The track count is read from GlobalData each time; the edge limit
///   is the one the system holds.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The track count is the GlobalData value that BFME 2's field table fills
/// from MaxTerrainTracks in GameData.ini, so raising it grows this buffer.
/// Zero Hour's version asserts, in debug builds only, that tracks times
/// edges times two stays below 65,535 ("Too many terrain track edges");
/// the retail BFME 2 body checks nothing.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This is one of the resource owners a renderer port must rebuild when its
/// device or swap chain is lost. The index layout assumes 16-bit indices.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's body is the same apart from that assert, and BFME 2
///       clears each pointer inside its release test.
/// \see TerrainTracksRenderObjClassSystem::flush,
///      BaseHeightMapRenderObjClass::ReAcquireResources,
///      DX8Wrapper::Reset_Device


/// \class ScreenMotionBlurFilter
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_w3d_render_stats_device
/// \brief The screen filter that blurs the tactical view for the camera's
///        zoom, jump-cut and pan motion-blur effects.
///
/// A screen filter (W3DFilterInterface) reworks the rendered view as a
/// whole. W3DShaderManager keeps a table of them indexed by filter type,
/// which this filter's init writes itself into. Zero Hour: W3DView selects
/// a filter type and a filter mode, and the shader manager runs the
/// selected filter's hooks around the scene render. BFME 2's vtable for
/// this class has Zero Hour's slot order: init, shutdown, preRender,
/// postRender, setup, set and reset.
///
/// \par How it works
/// - Init enters the filter in W3DShaderManager's table, in the slot Zero
///   Hour assigns to the motion-blur filter type, but only when
///   W3DShaderManager::canRenderToTexture reports support; otherwise it
///   fails and the filter stays unavailable. (BFME 2 body.)
/// - ScreenMotionBlurFilter::setup runs once when the effect starts and
///   translates the filter mode into the working settings.
/// - ScreenMotionBlurFilter::preRender redirects the scene into a render
///   texture, and ScreenMotionBlurFilter::postRender draws that texture
///   back over the view as a quad, then up to 30 fainter, slightly
///   rescaled copies of it, which make the blur. The zoom effects shrink
///   the quad's texture coordinates about the screen centre by a counter.
///   An in mode counts up by 5 once per logic frame and the effect ends
///   when it reaches 60. An out mode starts at 60 and counts down by 5 per
///   logic frame. An in-and-out mode turns at 60 and counts back down only
///   when a zoom-to position has been stored (set by
///   ScreenMotionBlurFilter::setZoomToPos): the camera then moves there.
///   Without a stored position the effect ends at 60. The pan modes size
///   the blur from the camera's scroll distance and the pan factor, and
///   the end-of-pan mode counts the blur down along the last scroll
///   direction.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// Zero Hour: one static instance in W3DShaderManager.cpp, entered in the
/// filter table by its init, for the life of the shader manager. The
/// zoom-to position is a static member shared by all users.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Map scripts start and stop the effect; the action names are listed on
/// the group page. Zero Hour's four motion-blur script actions map onto
/// the filter modes: the zoom action picks one of the in or out, alpha or
/// saturated modes, the jump-cut action an in-and-out mode with the
/// waypoint as zoom-to position, the follow action the pan mode plus the
/// script's amount, and the end-follow action the end-of-pan mode. That
/// BFME 2's script actions build the modes the same way is not established
/// here.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The effect depends on render-to-texture and on drawing a screen-space
/// quad with the captured frame. A renderer port must provide both.
/// Without render-to-texture the filter's init fails and the effect is
/// unavailable.


/// \fn Bool ScreenMotionBlurFilter::setup(enum FilterModes mode)
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_w3d_render_stats_device
/// \brief Prepares the motion-blur filter for a new effect: chooses
///        additive or alpha blending, zoom-to behaviour, the pan factor and
///        the starting counter for the given mode.
///
/// It runs through the filter's vtable. Zero Hour: W3DShaderManager's
/// filterSetup calls it through the filter table, and filterSetup's only
/// callers are W3DView's setViewFilter and setViewFilterMode, which
/// call it whenever the change leaves both a filter and a mode selected and
/// keep the view's previous setting if setup fails. This setup never fails.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For the motion-blur modes the body sets:
/// - additive blending for the saturated modes (in-and-out, in and out):
///   postRender then adds the captured frame onto the screen, which
///   brightens towards white; every other mode uses ordinary alpha
///   blending;
/// - zoom-to for the two in-and-out modes, the alpha one and the saturated
///   one, so that at the turning point the camera moves to the zoom-to
///   position; without a stored position the effect ends at the turning
///   point;
/// - for the pan modes, the pan factor: the mode's distance above the base
///   pan mode value, or the default of 30 when that distance is zero. A
///   mode below the pan range leaves the previous pan factor in place;
/// - the counter is reset to zero for every mode except the end-of-pan
///   mode, which keeps counting down from the running pan;
/// - the out modes, alpha and saturated, start at the maximum count of 60
///   and count down, so they begin fully blurred and unwind.
///
/// \param mode The filter mode. Values at or above the base pan mode value
///             encode a pan with its strength.
/// \return true, always.
///
/// \note The logic is Zero Hour's line for line, but BFME 2's numeric
///       filter mode values differ. Matching the branch structure to Zero
///       Hour's shows the motion-blur modes shifted up by one and the pan
///       mode by two, which implies BFME 2 inserts one mode value before
///       the motion-blur block and one more before the pan mode. Which
///       modes were inserted is not established.
/// \see ScreenMotionBlurFilter::postRender, ScreenMotionBlurFilter::setZoomToPos


/// \fn void PixelScreenToW3DLogicalScreen(Int screenX, Int screenY, Real *logX, Real *logY, Int screenWidth, Int screenHeight)
/// \brief Converts a pixel position, with (0,0) at the top left, into W3D
///        logical screen coordinates, with (-1,-1) at the bottom left and
///        (1,1) at the top right.
///
/// W3D cameras work in this normalised space; CameraClass::Un_Project turns
/// a logical point back into a world-space point on the camera's view
/// plane. The callers include two that build pick rays this way:
/// - W3DView::getPickRay passes the position minus the view's pixel origin,
///   with the view's width and height, so the result is relative to the
///   tactical view.
/// - W3DMouse::draw passes the cursor position with the whole display's
///   width and height, then unprojects the result the same way.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// logX is 2 times screenX divided by screenWidth, minus 1. logY is the
/// same for screenY and screenHeight, negated, because pixel rows grow
/// downwards and logical y grows upwards. The arithmetic is in single
/// precision. There is no half-pixel offset, so pixel (0,0) maps to the
/// top-left corner of the screen, not to the centre of that pixel, and
/// nothing guards against a zero width or height.
///
/// \param screenX      Pixel x, relative to whatever rectangle
///                     screenWidth describes.
/// \param screenY      Pixel y, growing downwards.
/// \param logX         [out] Logical x, -1 at the left edge, 1 at the right.
/// \param logY         [out] Logical y, 1 at the top edge, -1 at the bottom.
/// \param screenWidth  Width of the rectangle in pixels; must not be 0.
/// \param screenHeight Height of the rectangle in pixels; must not be 0.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This is the resolution seam for picking: whatever pixel space the input
/// layer reports, the caller must pass the matching width and height.
/// Values outside the rectangle give logical values outside -1 to 1
/// without complaint.
///
/// \note Identical to Zero Hour's and BFME 1's versions.
/// \see W3DView::getPickRay, W3DMouse::draw, W3DLogicalScreenToPixelScreen
